---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Résumé. Kishore Sudhamalla" description="Product Designer with 16+ years across design and nearly 9 years in enterprise HR technology.">

<style>
  /* ── Screen styles ───────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .rs-wrap {
    max-width: 860px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1.5rem 2rem 5rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  /* Download control.
     Previously absolute at the sheet's top-right, which landed it on top of
     the 16px corner radius and crowded the name. It now sits in its own row
     above the sheet, the way a document toolbar does, so the sheet edge stays
     unbroken. `.dl-bar` is already hidden by the print block. */
  .dl-bar {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    margin-bottom: 0.875rem;
  }
  .dl-btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
    background: var(--color-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 100px;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.95rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color 0.2s ease, border-color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .dl-btn:hover { color: var(--color-ink); border-color: var(--color-ink); }

  .sheet {
    --color-ink: #0d0d0c;
    --color-ink-2: #60605a;
    --color-ink-3: #72726c;
    --color-border: #e8e8e2;
  }
  .sheet {
    background: #ffffff;
    border: 0.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 16px;
    padding: 3.5rem 3.5rem 3.5rem;
    color: #0d0d0c;
    font-family: 'Inter', ui-sans-serif, sans-serif;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.06);
  }

  /* Header */
  .r-header {
    display: grid;
    /* Single column on phones: the fixed `1fr auto` pushed the contact block
       past the right edge and made the whole page scroll sideways. */
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    align-items: start;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    border-bottom: 0.5px solid #e8e8e2;
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .r-header { grid-template-columns: 1fr auto; align-items: end; gap: 2rem; }
  }
  .r-ident { display: flex; align-items: center; }
  .r-name {
    font-size: 2.25rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    line-height: 1;
    color: #0d0d0c;
    margin: 0 0 0.25rem;
  }
  .r-title {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: #60605a;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
  .r-contacts {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: 0.25rem;
  }
  .r-contact-line {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: #60605a;
    text-decoration: none;
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }
  .r-contact-line:hover { color: #0d0d0c; }

  /* Philosophy */
  .r-philosophy {
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    color: #0d0d0c;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
    max-width: 560px;
  }

  /* Impact bar */
  .r-impact {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 0;
    border: 0.5px solid #e8e8e2;
    border-radius: 10px;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }
  .r-impact-cell {
    padding: 0.875rem 1rem;
    border-right: 0.5px solid #e8e8e2;
  }
  .r-impact-cell:last-child { border-right: none; }
  .r-impact-num {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    color: #0d0d0c;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  .r-impact-label {
    font-size: 0.5625rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #72726c;   /* AA: 4.84:1 on white */
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  /* Section */
  /* company context — one line, keeps the reader oriented without a paragraph */
  .r-role-note {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
  }
  .r-role-text strong { font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-ink); }

  .r-section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .r-section-label {
    font-size: 0.5625rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #0d0d0c;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 0.5px solid #e8e8e2;
  }

  /* Role rows */
  .r-role {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7rem 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .r-role-meta {
    padding-top: 0.1rem;
  }
  .r-role-company {
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #0d0d0c;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-bottom: 0.15rem;
  }
  .r-role-date {
    font-size: 0.5625rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: #72726c;   /* AA: 4.84:1 on white */
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .r-role-body {}
  .r-role-title {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #0d0d0c;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  .r-role-text {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #4a4a45;   /* 8.91:1 */
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
  }
  /* Consecutive paragraphs inside one role were butting together into a wall.
     Set on the adjacent sibling so a role's first line still sits tight under
     its title. */
  .r-role-text + .r-role-text { margin-top: 0.55rem; }
  .r-role-divider {
    height: 0.5px;
    background: #e8e8e2;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 1rem 8.5rem;
  }

  /* Sub-role (same company, different title) */
  /* Sub-roles share the role grid so every date lands in the same column.
     The nesting is shown by a rule on the body, not by indenting the row,
     which previously pushed sub-roles 1rem out of alignment. */
  .r-subrole {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7rem 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .r-subrole .r-role-body {
    padding-left: 1rem;
    border-left: 1.5px solid #e8e8e2;
  }

  /* Skills + Education two-col */
  /* Skills and Education used to sit side by side, which made two entries of
     education look sparse next to four groups of skills. Each is now a
     full-width band and the groups inside it flow in two columns, so both
     sections fill their row and neither is judged against the other. */
  .r-bottom > .r-section { margin-bottom: 1.75rem; }
  .r-bottom > .r-section:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .r-cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    column-gap: 2rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .r-cols { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
  /* Groups bind internally and separate externally: the gap below a name is
     now a sixth of the gap between groups, so four entries read as four
     blocks rather than eight evenly spaced lines. */
  .r-skills-group {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .r-skills-group-name {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: #0d0d0c;
    margin-bottom: 0.15rem;
  }
  .r-skills-list {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #4a4a45;   /* 8.91:1 */
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .r-rec {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    column-gap: 2rem;
    row-gap: 0.4rem;
  }
  .r-rec li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 0.8rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #4a4a45;
  }
  .r-rec li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.62em;
    width: 4px;
    height: 1px;
    background: #a3a39c;
  }
  .r-rec em { font-style: italic; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .r-rec { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }

  /* Personal note */
  .r-personal {
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 0.5px solid #e8e8e2;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #72726c;   /* AA: 4.84:1 on white */
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* ── Print styles ────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  @media print {
    @page {
      size: A4;
      margin: 1.4cm 1.5cm;
    }

    body { background: #fff !important; }

    nav, footer, .dl-bar { display: none !important; }

    .rs-wrap {
      max-width: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0;
    }

    .sheet {
      border: none;
      border-radius: 0;
      padding: 0;
      box-shadow: none;
    }

    /* Measured content is ~1002px against a 1017px A4 text block, but Chromium
       still spilled a hair onto page two. A small print-only scale removes the
       rounding risk entirely and is imperceptible at reading size. */
    /* Two pages by design — 16 years does not compress to one without
       throwing away the evidence that does the work. */
    .sheet { zoom: 1; }

    .r-name { font-size: 1.35rem; }
    .r-impact-num { font-size: 0.86rem; }

    /* ── One page, on purpose ──────────────────────────────────────────
       The screen layout is deliberately airy; print has a hard budget of
       261mm. Everything below is spacing and type scale only — nothing is
       hidden, so the PDF and the web page say exactly the same thing. */
    .r-title        { font-size: 0.76rem; }
    .r-philosophy   { font-size: 0.73rem; line-height: 1.42; margin: 0.4rem 0 0.45rem; }
    .r-header       { margin-bottom: 0.3rem; padding-bottom: 0.3rem; }
    .r-contact-line { font-size: 0.67rem; line-height: 1.38; }

    .r-impact       { margin: 0.25rem 0; padding: 0.2rem 0; }
    .r-impact-label { font-size: 0.55rem; line-height: 1.22; }

    .r-section       { margin-top: 0.9rem; }
    .r-section-label { font-size: 0.58rem; margin-bottom: 0.35rem; padding-bottom: 0.25rem; }

    .r-role         { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
    .r-subrole      { margin-top: 0.6rem; }
    .r-role-company { font-size: 0.74rem; }
    .r-role-date    { font-size: 0.68rem; }
    .r-role-title   { font-size: 0.74rem; margin-bottom: 0.1rem; }
    .r-role-text    { font-size: 0.78rem; line-height: 1.5; }
    .r-role-text + .r-role-text { margin-top: 0.3rem; }
    .r-role-divider { margin: 0.3rem 0; }

    .r-bottom            { margin-top: 0.4rem; }
    .r-bottom > .r-section { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
    .r-cols              { column-gap: 1.35rem; }
    .r-rec    { row-gap: 0.2rem; column-gap: 1.35rem; }
    .r-rec li { font-size: 0.72rem; line-height: 1.45; }
    .r-skills-group      { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
    .r-skills-group-name { font-size: 0.72rem; margin-bottom: 0.05rem; }
    .r-skills-list       { font-size: 0.72rem; line-height: 1.5; }


    a { color: inherit; text-decoration: none; }

    * { -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact; print-color-adjust: exact; }
  }
</style>

<div class="rs-wrap">

  <!-- Download bar -->
  <div class="dl-bar no-print">
    <button class="dl-btn" onclick="window.print()">
      <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 13 13" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6.5 1v7M3.5 5.5l3 3 3-3M1.5 11h10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
      Save as PDF
    </button>
  </div>

  <!-- ══ Resume sheet ══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="sheet">

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="r-header">
      <div class="r-ident">
        <div>
          <h1 class="r-name">Kishore Sudhamalla</h1>
          <p class="r-title">Product Designer · Complex Enterprise Systems · AI &amp; Agentic Experiences</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="r-contacts">
        <a class="r-contact-line" href="mailto:build@kishoresudhamalla.com">build@kishoresudhamalla.com</a>
        <span class="r-contact-line">+91 99120 86069</span>
        <a class="r-contact-line" href="https://linkedin.com/in/kishuxer" target="_blank">linkedin.com/in/kishuxer</a>
        <a class="r-contact-line" href="https://kishoresudhamalla.com" target="_blank">kishoresudhamalla.com</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Philosophy statement -->
    <p class="r-philosophy">
      I design complex enterprise products and build what I design. Sixteen years of
      making difficult systems usable, nine of them on a global HR platform, which is
      where I learned to work with years of accumulated history, many user roles, and
      business rules most people never see. That same systems thinking is now going into
      AI and agentic experiences: what the system decides, what it suggests, and what
      stays with a person.
    </p>

    <!-- Impact bar -->
    <div class="r-impact" data-count-group>
      <div class="r-impact-cell">
        <div class="r-impact-num" data-count>16+ yrs</div>
        <div class="r-impact-label">Design<br>experience</div>
      </div>
      <div class="r-impact-cell">
        <div class="r-impact-num" data-count>~9 yrs</div>
        <div class="r-impact-label">Enterprise<br>platform depth</div>
      </div>
      <div class="r-impact-cell">
        <div class="r-impact-num" data-count>6</div>
        <div class="r-impact-label">Product areas<br>owned</div>
      </div>
      <div class="r-impact-cell">
        <div class="r-impact-num" data-count>Global</div>
        <div class="r-impact-label">US · SEA · MENA<br>products shipped</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Experience -->
    <div class="r-section">
      <div class="r-section-label">Experience</div>

      <!-- Darwinbox, the spine of the resume -->
      <div class="r-role">
        <div class="r-role-meta">
          <div class="r-role-company">Darwinbox</div>
          <div class="r-role-date">2017 – Present</div>
          <div class="r-role-note">Global HCM platform</div>
        </div>
        <div class="r-role-body">
          <div class="r-role-title">Associate Director, UX</div>
          <p class="r-role-text">
            Leading AI-native and agentic experience design across the platform, defining
            where AI should act on someone's behalf, where it should assist, and where
            humans need to remain in control. Designing the behaviours, interactions and
            guardrails around autonomy, permissions, explainability, trust, failure and
            recovery.
          </p>
          <p class="r-role-text">
            Setting design direction across six product areas: Time Management, Core,
            Payroll, Benefits, Extensibility, and Workflows &amp; Journeys, while staying
            hands-on with the hardest product problems and shaping how an enterprise HR
            platform evolves toward AI-native experiences.
          </p>
          <p class="r-role-text">
            Lead and mentor designers across multiple product areas while partnering with
            product and engineering leaders to shape product direction, resolve difficult
            product problems and raise design quality across teams.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="r-subrole">
        <div class="r-role-meta"><div class="r-role-date">2021 – 2024</div></div>
        <div class="r-role-body">
        <div class="r-role-title">Senior UX Manager</div>
        <p class="r-role-text">
          Rebuilt Time Management, an eight-year-old enterprise module that had become
          increasingly difficult to use as Darwinbox expanded across customers and markets.
          Reorganised the experience around what managers need to understand and decide
          first, rather than exposing the complexity of the underlying product structure.
          Worked through complex workforce rules, configurations, permissions and global
          requirements to create a clearer experience for different customer contexts.
          Validated the new model with 8 enterprise clients across Southeast Asia before
          global rollout. The work contributed to the evolution of Darwinbox's Workforce
          Management product, which <strong>moved from 2.9 → 3.5 in the Gartner Magic
          Quadrant for Workforce Management by 2025</strong>.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="r-subrole">
        <div class="r-role-meta"><div class="r-role-date">2017 – 2021</div></div>
        <div class="r-role-body">
        <div class="r-role-title">UX Manager · Senior Product Designer</div>
        <p class="r-role-text">
          Joined Darwinbox as an <strong>early founding member of the design team</strong>
          and helped establish the foundations for product design as the company scaled
          rapidly. Built the initial design language and style guide, introduced reusable
          patterns and established a design review practice that helped the team maintain
          product quality without losing the speed a startup needs.
        </p>
        <p class="r-role-text">
          Led the design of Workflows &amp; Journeys, a platform layer connecting HR events
          such as onboarding, transfers, promotions and exits to the systems that execute
          them across country rules, business logic and role permissions. Designed for
          complex enterprise scenarios spanning multiple products, user roles and
          dependencies, and shipped the experience across 120+ countries. Worked closely
          with product and engineering to turn a complex platform model into a framework
          organisations could use to create and manage employee journeys.
        </p>
        <p class="r-role-text">
          This period covered Darwinbox's evolution from an early-stage startup into a
          unicorn, while the product, customer base and design organisation grew
          significantly alongside the business.
        </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="r-role-divider"></div>

      <!-- Earlier, compressed, enough to hold the 16-year line -->
      <div class="r-role">
        <div class="r-role-meta">
          <div class="r-role-company">WTA Studios</div>
          <div class="r-role-date">2015 – 2017</div>
        </div>
        <div class="r-role-body">
          <div class="r-role-title">Product Designer</div>
          <p class="r-role-text">
            End-to-end design for web and mobile products. Introduced design thinking to
            cross-functional teams and began mentoring junior designers.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="r-role-divider"></div>

      <div class="r-role">
        <div class="r-role-meta">
          <div class="r-role-company">Earlier roles</div>
          <div class="r-role-date">2009 – 2015</div>
        </div>
        <div class="r-role-body">
          <div class="r-role-title">UI/UX · Visual · Graphic Designer</div>
          <p class="r-role-text">
            Wave Crest Group (enterprise dashboards), Green Gold Animation (Chhota Bheem
            franchise), Anil Bhanu Core Design. Six years across every medium before
            specialising in product.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Selected build -->
    <div class="r-section">
      <div class="r-section-label">Designed and built solo</div>
      <div class="r-role">
        <div class="r-role-meta">
          <div class="r-role-company">Design Ops</div>
          <div class="r-role-date">2026</div>
          <div class="r-role-note">Side project · in daily use</div>
        </div>
        <div class="r-role-body">
          <div class="r-role-title">A delivery system for a design org: design through to working product</div>
          <p class="r-role-text">
            Jira reported tickets, not whether a team was on pace. I designed and built a tool
            that syncs a live Jira board and reports delivery, capacity and slipped commitments. Vanilla JavaScript, no framework, written in Claude Code. In daily use.
          </p>
          <p class="r-role-text">
            Then added the layer I actually wanted to design: an AI that explains <em>why</em> a
            commitment slipped, cites the changelog entries and comments it read, proposes one
            action, and only writes to Jira after a person approves the exact record. It measures
            whether the intervention held, and reports its own recommendations when they failed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Bottom: full-width bands, each flowing in two columns -->
    <div class="r-bottom">
      <div class="r-section">
        <div class="r-section-label">Skills</div>
        <div class="r-cols">
          <div class="r-skills-group">
            <div class="r-skills-group-name">Complex systems</div>
            <div class="r-skills-list">Enterprise UX · systems thinking · platform design · workflows &amp; journeys · multi-role permissions · global rollouts</div>
          </div>
          <div class="r-skills-group">
            <div class="r-skills-group-name">AI &amp; agentic UX</div>
            <div class="r-skills-list">Human–AI responsibility models · explainability and evidence · confidence and uncertainty · human-in-the-loop approval · failure and recovery</div>
          </div>
          <div class="r-skills-group">
            <div class="r-skills-group-name">Research &amp; direction</div>
            <div class="r-skills-list">Jobs to Be Done · card sorting · enterprise client validation · design sprints · stakeholder alignment</div>
          </div>
          <div class="r-skills-group">
            <div class="r-skills-group-name">Prototyping &amp; building</div>
            <div class="r-skills-list">Claude Code · Figma · Figma MCP · Adobe CC</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="r-section">
        <div class="r-section-label">Education &amp; Certifications</div>
        <div class="r-cols">
          <div class="r-skills-group">
            <div class="r-skills-group-name">ISBM</div>
            <div class="r-skills-list">Bachelor's in Visual Communications</div>
          </div>
          <div class="r-skills-group">
            <div class="r-skills-group-name">Xperian · IBM</div>
            <div class="r-skills-list">Digital Product Design · Enterprise Design Thinking · Essentials for AI</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="r-section r-section--recognition">
        <div class="r-section-label">Recognition &amp; Speaking</div>
        <ul class="r-rec">
          <li>Five Best Contributor of the Quarter awards at Darwinbox</li>
          <li>Best Graphic Designer of the Year, <em>Chhota Bheem and the Curse of Damyaan</em></li>
          <li>Presented at the SEA UX Roadshow across Singapore, the Philippines &amp; Thailand</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Personal note -->
    <p class="r-personal">
      I've always learned by making: a product concept, a visual experiment, an
      AI prototype, or a new tool I'm trying to understand. Always open to
      conversations about complex products, enterprise systems, and AI-driven
      product experiences.
    </p>

  </div>
</div>

</Base>
